<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Soria</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Soria is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and León, north-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, capital of the province of the same name on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Douro</a>. The province covers the heartland of the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arevaci">Arevaci</a>, including the ruins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numantia">Numantia</a> just north of the city. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> Soria was a Castilian frontier town and a centre of the wool trade, and the poet Antonio Machado lived and taught there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/soria.jpg" alt="Soria" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: FRANCIS RAHER · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Soria_-_P7234619_edited.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Soria sits on the banks of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Douro</a>, at about 1,065 metres above sea level, which makes it the second highest provincial capital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Its oceanic climate has very cold winters by Spanish standards and scant rainfall. The name may come from dauria, a form of the name of the river Durius, the Douro. Older explanations, that it descended from a castle named Oria after a Greek knight called Doricus, or that the first inhabitants were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorians">Dorians</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suebi">Suebi</a>, have been abandoned for lack of evidence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first recorded inhabitants of the area were the Celtiberians, around the 4th century BCE. After the Arab conquest the town grew in importance because it lay near the border of the Christian lands. In 869 it was the centre of a rebellion by Suleyman ibn-Abus against the emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>. Soria itself enters history with its repopulation between 1109 and 1114 by the Aragonese king Alfonso I the Battler, who held the territory until 1134. It received a short fuero in 1120, confirmed by Alfonso VII of León in 1143.</p><p>Because Soria lay where several kingdoms met, it was at the centre of medieval conflicts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, who was born there, rewarded its support with privileges that it kept into modern times, and Alfonso X held his court there when he received the offer of the throne of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. It prospered in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> through its border location and its control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> industry, but lost importance after the union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> and Castile in 1479 and the decree of exile of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in 1492. French troops captured it and set it on fire in 1808.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Soria is a city and municipality in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and León and the capital of its province. Its heritage includes extensive medieval walls, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> palaces and distinctive Romanesque churches such as San Juan de Rabanera. A few kilometres to the north lie the ruins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numantia">Numantia</a>, a Celtiberian town whose inhabitants destroyed it rather than let it fall to Scipio, and the Numantine Museum holds finds from it. Antonio Machado spent five years teaching French in a secondary school in Soria, an experience that shaped his poetry.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Soria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Celtiberians were a group of Celtic-speaking peoples of the eastern Meseta, the high plateau of central Spain, in the upper basins of the Ebro's southern tributaries, the Douro and the Tagus, roughly the modern provinces of <b>Soria</b>, Guadalajara, Zaragoza, Teruel and Cuenca.</blockquote><a href="/empire/celtiberians">Celtiberians · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Arevaci occupied the upper Douro basin in modern <b>Soria</b> and Segovia from about the 4th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/arevaci">Arevaci</a></li><li><blockquote>The Pellendones lived around the sources of the Douro in northern <b>Soria</b> and southern La Rioja from about the 4th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/pellendones">Pellendones</a></li></ul></section>
